  {
    "episode_id": 85641,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85641.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, what I did was ask people the very common thing to find a given country on a map. And I did it with a series of surveys where each person had to find 10 countries on a map. And then I collated all that data. and made a what they call a cartogram which is one of those distorted maps like you give for like electoral college results or something. But I distorted this map according to what percentage of the American population could not find a given country on a map. So Americans are pretty good at finding, you know, America. And so you can see that. But they are really lousy finding countries in Africa. So on this map, you know, Africa looks very distorted, very large. But you'd be surprised, too, aside from a few countries in Europe, like France, England, Germany, Americans are really are not good at finding Switzerland, Greece, Austria, you know. Now, when I first did this, I thought, okay, Americans are really stupid with maps. That's established. But I did a British version of this book where I wrote a fair amount of new material and conducted surveys with the British public. And I found that the Britons were really just as bad with maps as we are. Now, they were better at finding European countries, but aside from that, they were just as bad as Americans. So I kind of suspect that this tends to be a global phenomenon that a lot of people just don't get the idea of a map. They don't have this visual intelligence, a sort of mental image of the world. And that makes it very hard to really understand how countries relate to the US and to what's going on in the world.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
