  {
    "episode_id": 85641,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85641.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Are people getting smarter? Well, that's a good question and I'm not even sure that my research can really answer it because obviously all my surveys were done within a fairly narrow time range. I can't say this, though. I mean, if you go back Socrates, complain that the people were getting dumber, the younger generation back in archaic Greece was dumber than the older generation. So if that perception had been true, I mean, we'd have the IQ of a houseplant nowadays. And we're a little better than that. So I think it's just sort of natural in human nature to think, all these kids today, they don't know all these things we were taught in school, so they have to be done. But I think the reality is that the culture is always changing. I mean, just in science and technology, there are so many things that kids are being taught now that we're not taught 20 years ago. It's common to have coding classes in school now, and this was not common in their parents' time. So you have to, before saying that young people are just getting stupider and stupider, you have to realize that they are learning an awful lot of things that maybe older people never did learn.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
